<sn><md>### Single-Line Comments

A `/* */` comment can be written on a single line:

```c
/* this is a comment */
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Multi-Line Comments

`/* */` comments can span multiple lines:

```c
/* this is a
multi-line
comment */
```

A common style convention is to place `/*` and `*/` on separate lines and use an asterisk at the beginning of each line:

```c
/*
 * this is a
 * multi-line
 * comment
 */
```

The additional `*` characters have **no special functional meaning**. They are simply used to improve readability.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Where `/* */` Comments Can Be Used

These comments can appear:

1. On their own line
2. At the end of a line of code
3. Within a line of code
4. Inside a block such as an `if` statement

```c
/* this comment is on its own line */

if (x &amp;&amp; y) { /* this comment is at the end of a line */

    if ((complexCondition1) /* this comment is within a line of code */
        &amp;&amp; (complexCondition2)) {

        /* this comment is within an if, on its own line */
    }
}
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Comments Cannot Be Nested

`/* */` comments **cannot be nested**.

If another `/*` appears inside an existing comment, it is treated as ordinary text. The compiler continues searching for the **first `*/`** it encounters, which ends the comment.

For example:

```c
/*
 * outer comment
 * /* attempted inner comment */
 * this is no longer part of the comment
 */
```

The first `*/` closes the outer comment. The final `*/` is therefore left outside the comment and can cause a compilation error.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Commenting Out Code Containing Comments

Because `/* */` comments cannot be nested, you should not use them to comment out a block of code that already contains `/* */` comments.

For example:

```c
/*
int foo()
{
    /* This comment is already inside the code */
    return 1;
}
*/
```

This will **not work as intended**.

For such cases, use the preprocessor technique:

```c
#if 0

int foo()
{
    /* This comment can remain here */
    return 1;
}

#endif
```

The `#if 0` / `#endif` technique allows an entire section of code to be excluded by the preprocessor without creating nested C comments.
</md></sn>